{"author":{"id":"f21006d1826991d76b358f177209294474e8c2701fc659ff8fe8b8f40ee77f55"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1748278874,"nonce":"iGJ0zSok6+620LIjLBrcguT9Zl8=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOCGKeh86td1Sm","github-url":"https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug/issues/1139#issuecomment-2910278822"},"message":"@csabaxyz `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` can be an arbitrary value; this is just a  path to _a_ socket, and doesn't need to be the same for everyone.\n\n\u003e I'm not sure if I'm supposed to be prompted to add the key if it is missing from the agent, but I wasn't asked.\n\nAs you experienced, manually adding your key to the agent (which effectively caches it) will work, since the agent already has the key. Automatically prompting you for your passphrase is not something that ssh-agent supports, you must manually add your key to the agent -- e.g. see guides on [the archi linux wiki](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/SSH_keys#ssh-agent) or [github](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/generating-a-new-ssh-key-and-adding-it-to-the-ssh-agent).\n\nIn [this comment](https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug/issues/1139#issuecomment-2867881532) by @MartyLake, we see that the value for the `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` variable is set to `/run/user/1000/gnupg/S.gpg-agent.ssh`, which indicates that they are using `gpg-agent` as their ssh key agent. I do this, too -- this is how we're automatically prompted for our passphrase; this is something that `gpg-agent` supports.","files":null},{"type":6,"timestamp":1748279212,"nonce":"D4z7v6yIRWXySC8MbPHXySFgn98=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lALOCGKeh86td1Smzmhxu3s"},"target":"5c245546f919789a97764722cc64a6d4eb4d31624361454d9ced64cb753cb010","message":"@csabaxyz `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` can be an arbitrary value; this is just a  path to _a_ socket, and doesn't need to be the same for everyone.\n\n\u003e I'm not sure if I'm supposed to be prompted to add the key if it is missing from the agent, but I wasn't asked.\n\nAs you experienced, manually adding your key to the agent (which effectively caches it) will work, since the agent already has the key. Automatically prompting you for your passphrase is not something that ssh-agent supports, you must manually add your key to the agent -- e.g. see guides on [the archi linux wiki](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/SSH_keys#ssh-agent) or [github](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/generating-a-new-ssh-key-and-adding-it-to-the-ssh-agent).\n\nIn [this comment](https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug/issues/1139#issuecomment-2867881532) by @MartyLake, we see that the value for the `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` variable is set to `/run/user/1000/gnupg/S.gpg-agent.ssh`, which indicates that they are using `gpg-agent` as their ssh key agent. I do this, too -- this is how we're automatically prompted for our passphrase; this is something that `gpg-agent` supports.\n\n---\n\n\u003e _this is how we're automatically prompted for our passphrase; this is something that gpg-agent supports._\n\ni should clarify that under normal circumstances without adding your key to the agent, when you run `ssh user@host` or do a `git push`, you _would_ be prompted for your passphrase in the terminal -- it's not that you wouldn't be prompted at all, it's just a matter of how that prompt is implemented. gpg-agent invokes pinentry, which becomes a blocking process on my terminal. natively, openssh is just going to ask for your passphrase in a `read`-like prompt, which doesn't take over the terminal, and isn't something that git-bug can handle (today).","files":null}]}